="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Carbon generally comes from serine.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p><a:p><a:pPr indent="-342900" lvl="0" marL="342900" marR="0" rtl="0" algn="l"><a:lnSpc><a:spcPct val="100000"/></a:lnSpc><a:spcBef><a:spcPts val="560"/></a:spcBef><a:spcAft><a:spcPts val="0"/></a:spcAft><a:buClr><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:buClr><a:buSzPts val="2800"/><a:buFont typeface="Calibri"/><a:buChar char="•"/></a:pPr><a:r><a:rPr b="0" i="0" lang="en-US" sz="2800" u="none"><a:solidFill><a:schemeClr val="dk1"/></a:solidFill><a:latin typeface="Calibri"/><a:ea typeface="Calibri"/><a:cs typeface="Calibri"/><a:sym typeface="Calibri"/></a:rPr><a:t>Forms are interconverted on THF before use.</a:t></a:r><a:endParaRPr/></a:p></p:txBody></p:sp><p:pic><p:nvPicPr><p:cNvPr descr="To left is 6-methylpterin, which has a bracket underneath. This has two hexagons which share a side. The left hexagon has N at the top point. The bottom point double bonds down to O. The bottom left point is HN, and the top left point bonds up and right to H2N. Inside the hexagon the top point has a 1, the top left point has 2, the bottom left point has 3, and the bottom point has 4. It also has a double bond on the top left side. The right hexagon has a top point of H over N, the top right point bonds up and right to H and down and right to H. The bottom right point bonds up and right to H and down and right to CH2 (which has a 9 underneath). The bottom point has N (in blue) over H. Inside the hexagon the bottom point has 5, the bottom right has 6, and tope right point has 7, and the top point has 8. The shared side is double-bonded. Above this double bond is 8a and below is 4a. The CH2 from the right hexagon bonds right to NH (N is in blue) and has a 10 above. The NH begins p-aminobenzoate in a bracket. The NH bonds right to a hexagon with alternating sides, which bonds right to C. C double bonds up to O, which bonds right to NH. NH begins glutamate (in a bracket) and bonds right to CH, which bonds up to COO- and right to CH2, which bonds right to CH2, which bonds right to COO-. This entire cycle represents tetrahydrofolate (H4 folate). " id="462" name="Google Shape;462;p61"/><p:cNvPicPr preferRelativeResize="0"/><p:nvPr/></p:nvPicPr><p:blipFill rotWithShape="1"><a:blip r:embed="rId3"><a:alphaModFix/></a:blip><a:srcRect b="0" l="0" r="0" t="0"/><a:stretch/></p:blipFill><p:spPr><a:xfrm><a:off x="1219200" y="4313237"/><a:ext cx="6858000" cy="2468562"/></a:xfrm><a:prstGeom prst="rect"><a:avLst/></a:prstGeom><a:noFill/><a:ln><a:noFill/></a:ln></p:spPr></p:pic></p:spTree></p:cSld><p:clrMapOvr><a:masterClrMapping/></p:clrMapOvr></p:sld>